          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-reduced-khovanov-cohomology-of-a-knot",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(D,p)$ be a finite regular oriented diagram of a knot with a basepoint $p$ away from the crossings, and let $C(D)$ be its normalized integral Khovanov cochain complex. At each resolution state, let $X_p$ act by multiplication by $X$ on the tensor factor for the circle containing $p$, and by the identity on every other circle factor. The basepoint action is the direct sum of these statewise maps. Define the reduced cochain complex by\n$$\\widetilde C(D,p):=C(D)/\\operatorname{im}(X_p),$$\nwith the same cohomological grading and with reduced quantum degree $\\widetilde j=j-1$ for the class represented by a state whose basepoint circle is labelled $1$. Its cohomology is the reduced integral Khovanov cohomology $\\widetilde{Kh}^{i,\\widetilde j}(D,p)$. The unknot is normalized so that its reduced group lies in bidegree $(0,0)$.\n\nEquivalently, cut the based knot at $p$ to a one-interval tangle. Put $M=\\mathbb Z$ in quantum degree $0$ with $X\\cdot1=0$. A resolution with $k$ closed circles and the distinguished interval is assigned $\\mathcal A^{\\otimes k}\\otimes M$. An edge that merges a circle into the interval uses $m_M(1\\otimes1)=1$ and $m_M(X\\otimes1)=0$; an edge that splits the interval uses $\\Delta_M(1)=X\\otimes1$, where the circle factor is written first and the interval factor second. Edges not meeting the interval use $m$ or $\\Delta$. This based tangle cube is the quotient complex above, with the stated quantum shift.",
